{"chunk_id": "b56e3053db74", "text": "This section of the Bhagavad Gita presents Arjuna questioning Krishna about whether renouncing action or performing action is the superior path. Krishna explains that both renunciation (Sankhya) and disciplined action (Yoga) can lead to liberation, but acting without attachment to results is the better path for most people. True renunciation is not abandoning work but performing it without desire, ego, or claim to its fruits. The wise person understands that the senses act on the world while the Self remains untouched, like a lotus leaf untouched by water. Such a person sees all beings with equal vision, remains steady in pleasure and pain, and finds happiness within rather than in fleeting sensory pleasures. Self-mastery, knowledge of the Self, and devotion lead to union with Brahman and lasting tranquility.\nKrishna teaches that selfless action with detachment surpasses mere renunciation, leading to inner peace, equality toward all beings, and realization of Brahman.\nThemes: selfless action, renunciation vs action, inner equanimity, unity of Sankhya and Yoga, meditative self-mastery", "vector": [0.009462567046284676, -0.006927237845957279, -0.005067331250756979, 0.00040321549749933183, -0.020602045580744743, 0.008610802702605724, -0.02327711693942547, -0.013748005032539368, -0.030184391885995865, -0.004904298111796379, 0.00802521500736475, 0.02526012994349003, 0.014346902258694172, 0.02265160158276558, 0.0023589874617755413, -0.01072690449655056, 0.05626966804265976, -0.01268330030143261, 0.03391085937619209, -0.0016378159634768963, -0.00387951941229403, -0.007612641900777817, -0.00015783421986270696, -0.017674105241894722, 0.008238156326115131, 0.002302424982190132, 0.020429030060768127, -0.032899390906095505, 0.010287713259458542, -0.0208815298974514, -0.005945712793618441, 0.003653269726783037, -0.0007964493706822395, -0.01012800820171833, -0.03734453395009041, -0.010966463014483452, -0.004804482217878103, -0.0051671471446